ency and  so forth of different variants and as I  briefly mentioned the table and the  graph  will display our interactive such that  if you update the region that's shown in  the graphical display the content of the  table is going to change accordingly  other features that we're going to use  today are the pic assembly tool as  variation viewer supports content from  both grch37 also known as hg19 as well  as grch38 also known as hg 38 we're  going to use the search option to  navigate to regions of interest and  we're also going to use the section over  here called user data and track hubs in  order to add externally sourced data  into this browser so to illustrate the  different types of variant analyses that  you can do in variation viewer I'm going  to use the following hypothetical case  study that's described on this slide  here so you can imagine you're working  with a collaborator who gives you a VCF  file that has three suspect variants  from a patient who's exhibiting a  phenotype suggestive of severe combined  immunodeficiency also known as skid  suggesting that the ad a or adenosine  deaminase gene might be involved and  your collaborator also tells you that  this patient additionally suffers from  irritable bowel disease so today we'll  look at how we can use variation viewer  to evaluate and prioritize these three  variants that your collaborator gave you  and also to see if we can find any  evidence to suggest that the irritable  bowel disease is perhaps associated with  the skid Lake phenotype so next let's go  ahead and get variation viewer set up  for our analyses using the steps that  are outlined on this slide here first  we're going to go ahead and we're going  to select grch38 for our analyses as I  just mentioned previously variation  viewer does support analyses on both  assembly versions but for today in the  interest of time we're going to take the  approach that's described in this case  study here in which our collaborator  made their 