wn here  at the bottom highlighted with this  purple bracket you can see the VCF  derived track which we added last and  since I added it last it's at the bottom  of the display so now I can click  and dragged on this track to move it  directly beneath the genes track to  facilitate my analysis and the next few  steps so to navigate to the location of  any of the VCF calls and that file that  we were provided we can click on the  corresponding row in the table in the  user data and track hub section so we're  going to start our analysis today  actually with the second row call equals  G so I click on that and the browser  updates to that location and as I  mentioned our uploaded track has the  green highlight shown here so you can  see how I've now dragged it beneath the  the gains track as I mentioned before  and the thin line here in the genes  track again indicates that we're in the  intron of the a T of an intron in the a  da gene now while most of the other  tracks in our track set here are empty  we do see that there is data in the DB  snip all data track and if we blow this  up we can see that there is a snip rs-24  four zero seven four that's annotated at  the location of our call so to learn  more about this particular snip we're  going to make use of the variant table  down here at the bottom portion of  variation viewer as I mentioned that  table can be filtered by different  characteristics and right now I'm  showing in the table only the snips and  I'm excluding any structural variants  that might have come from the DB bar  database now in agreement with the  graphical display that we just looked at  the table tells us that the snip is  intronic and it also shows us that the  thousand genomes project found that the  associated a allele at this location has  a minor allele frequency of zero so at  this point we're going to go ahead and  remember that our collaborator made  their variant call on the grch37  assembly and we also remember that that  and genome allele frequencies are based  on grch37 so in this case the grch37  reference allele was an A and that's the  allele that had the allele frequency of  zero which is indicative of a possible  assembly area error in this earlier  version of the human reference genome  now I could update variation viewer to  look specifically at the grch37 content  to verify the a at this location but  again that allele frequency suggests  that this is an assembly error so  looking more carefully now at grch38 we  see that the reference assembly now has  a G at this possession which indicates  that there was an assembly update  between the two assembly versions so  based on these data we can conclude that  we're able to actually deep prioritize  this call from our collaborator because  the G that they saw is not actually  variant relative to the current  reference assembly so moving on line we  can use the user data table again to  look at another variant and in this case  we're going to go to the location of  call equals a so we click on that and  this is now the variant that is centered  in our graphical display and if we blow  this view up again here we can see that  once again we have a snip that's  annotated at the position of this call  but this time however a review of that  filtered variant table where I've got  that variant now highlighted in red  shows us that it's an exon ik missense  variant and that our call is also a  minor allele which in this case has a  nonzero allele frequency as determined  by the exact project so if we click here  to expand that row of the table we  actually get additional information  about the affected refseq transcripts  associated with this variant and we also  learn something about the amino acid  changes for the variants that fall in  exonic regions now in addition to  looking at the variant table we can also  notice that the  is content in two of our browser tracks  the clinvar short variations track and  the cited variants track so let's